time limit per test: 1 second
memory limit per test: 256 megabytes

You are given an array [a1, a2, ..., an]. You wish to make the array empty by performing the following operation any number of times:
Select any sequence of indices 1 <= i1 < i2 < ... < ik <= |a| (note that |a| denotes the current length of the array a) such that a_i1 <= a_i2 <= ... <= a_ik.
Remove the elements a_i1, a_i2, ..., a_ik from the array a.
This operation incurs a cost equal to a_i1 * a_i2 * ... * a_ik.
Determine the minimum total cost required to remove all the elements from the array a. Note that the total cost is equal to the sum of costs incurred over all the operations performed.
As the answer can be very large, report the answer modulo 676 767 677.

Input:
Each test contains multiple test cases. The first line contains the number of test cases t (1 <= t <= 500). The description of the test cases follows.
The first line of each testcase contains a single integer n (1 <= n <= 100) — the length of the array a.
The second line of each testcase contains n integers a1, a2, ..., an (1 <= ai <= 100) — the elements of the array.

Output:
For each testcase, output a single integer — the minimum total cost required to make the array a empty.
As the answer may be large, output the answer modulo 676 767 677.

Strategy:
The description is a bit clunky but we can delete any non-decreasing sequence of numbers in the array with the cost of those numbers multiplied. Since they multiply the cheapest is to remove them one by one but we need to watch for an edge case of 1. Since 1*x is 1 it would not be optimal to delete 1s with something therefore we need to append them to things. Since 1 is the smallest possible number we can append it to anything but because the sequence needs to be non decreasing if it is at the end we must count it separately. Therefore the total cost is the (sum of all non 1 numbers) +1 only if there is a 1 all the way at the end. We also check if there wasn't only 1s in the array (total==0) if so the answer is 1. We output that.